    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0092",
      "sequence": 104,
      "meaning": "Śambhu’s attendants adorn Śiva: they arrange a serpent as the crest upon his matted-hair crown; snakes serve as earrings and bracelets, ash covers his body, and a lion skin is his robe. The moon shines on his brow, Ganges crowns his head, three eyes and a serpent sacred-thread mark him; poison colors his throat and a garland of human skulls lies on his chest. Though his guise seems inauspicious, he is gracious Śiva, abode of auspiciousness. Holding trident and drum, he mounts the bull as instruments sound. Divine women smile that no bride in the world could suit such a groom. Viṣṇu, Brahmā and the gods mount their vehicles for the procession; the divine company is incomparable but does not resemble the bridegroom. Laughing, Viṣṇu tells the guardians of all directions to proceed separately with their own retinues.",
